UNIX(4)              UNIX Programmer's Manual               UNIX(4)



NAME
     unix - UNIX-domain protocol family

SYNOPSIS
     #include <sys/types.h>
     #include <sys/socket.h>
     #include <sys/un.h>

     s = socket(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
     s = socket(AF_UNIX, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);

DESCRIPTION
     The UNIX-domain protocol family connects sockets on one
     machine.  It uses the filesystem name space for addresses
     and no network of any kind: there is no protocol below it,
     nothing is serialised onto a wire, and a machine with no
     Ethernet card has the whole family available.  It is
     2.11BSD's, uipc_usrreq.c, in the network modules.

     SOCK_STREAM sockets are connected byte streams, with listen(2)
     and accept(2) as for TCP.  SOCK_DGRAM sockets carry records,
     each delivered whole or not at all.

ADDRESSING
     A UNIX-domain address is a pathname, carried in a
     sockaddr_un:

          struct sockaddr_un {
                  short   sun_family;     /* AF_UNIX */
                  char    sun_path[108];
          };

     Pass its length to bind(2), connect(2) and sendto(2):
     sizeof(struct sockaddr_un), or SUN_LEN.  A length sized as a
     16-byte sockaddr cuts the path at its thirteenth character,
     and bind(2) then names a different, shorter file.

     bind(2) creates a socket node on the filesystem, of type
     VSOCK: stat(2) reports it through S_ISSOCK and ls(1) shows it
     as a socket.  connect(2) and sendto(2) resolve a path to the
     socket bound to it and require write permission on the node,
     which is what makes chmod(1) on the name mean something.

     A BOUND SOCKET HOLDS ITS NODE'S VNODE for as long as it is
     open, and close(2) releases it.  close(2) does NOT remove the
     name -- a daemon that wants to rebind should unlink(2) first,
     which is what syslogd does -- so a socket node outliving its
     socket is the ordinary case after a restart.  Connecting to
     such a name answers ECONNREFUSED.

DIAGNOSTICS
     A socket(2) for a type the family has no protocol for, such
     as SOCK_RAW, answers EPROTONOSUPPORT.

     A bind(2) of a name that already exists answers EADDRINUSE.

     A connect(2) to a name that is not a socket answers ENOTSOCK;
     to a socket node whose socket has closed, or to a stream
     socket that is not listening, ECONNREFUSED; to a socket of
     the other type, EPROTOTYPE.

     A sendto(2) that names a path on a connected datagram socket
     answers EISCONN; on an unconnected one it connects for that
     datagram alone.

BUGS
     NO ACCESS RIGHTS.  Upstream passes descriptors between
     processes over a UNIX-domain socket, as access rights in a
     message.  Here a message carrying them answers EOPNOTSUPP: a
     descriptor is a kernel file record the network modules cannot
     name.  A port that expects to hand a descriptor to another
     process cannot do it this way.

     A SENDER'S NAME IS CUT TO 14 CHARACTERS.  recvfrom(2) and
     accept(2) copy at most 16 bytes of the peer's address, the
     size of a sockaddr, so a bound sender's path arrives cut at
     its fourteenth character.  An unbound sender's name is empty,
     as upstream's is.

SEE ALSO
     socket(2), bind(2), connect(2), listen(2), accept(2),
     send(2), recv(2), unlink(2), syslog(3), pipe(2)

HISTORY
     A UNIX-domain protocol family appeared in 4.2BSD.  This one
     is 2.11BSD's.
